<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Champawat</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Champawat is a town in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttarakhand">Uttarakhand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumaon">Kumaon</a> hills near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>. Tradition holds that it was the first seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chand-dynasty">Chand dynasty</a> of Kumaon, founded by Som Chand, before the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almora">Almora</a> in the 16th century. It is known for the carved stone <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balasore">Baleshwar</a> temple complex and is the headquarters of Champawat district.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/champawat.jpg" alt="Champawat" width="320" height="144" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: WhiteRaven335 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Champawat_town.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Champawat lies in the south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttarakhand">Uttarakhand</a>, in the eastern part of the Kumaon Himalayas, at an average elevation of 1,615 metres. Its historical name is said to be Champavati, after the Champavati stream on whose banks it stands. The valley of the stream holds seven ancient temples: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balasore">Baleshwar</a>, Kranteshwar, Tadkeshwar, Rishneshwar, Dikteshwar, Mallareshwar and Maneshwar. Of these the Baleshwar temple is considered the most important. According to a local epic, the Gurupaduka, Champavati, the sister of the Nagas, did penance near it, and a temple in her memory stands inside the Baleshwar complex.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Champawat was the original residence of the Rajas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumaon">Kumaon</a> before they moved their capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almora">Almora</a> in the mid-16th century, and it was formerly the capital of the Chand rulers. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balasore">Baleshwar</a> temple, built by the Chand rulers in the 12th century, is noted for its stone carving. To the west of the town stood a fort called Donkot, where the local Rawat kings lived. According to the Vayu Purana, Champavati was the capital of nine kings of the Puri Naga dynasty.</p><p>Local tradition tied to the Skanda Purana holds that a mound near the old fort is the Kurmachal, the spot where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> appeared in his Kurma, or tortoise, incarnation. The name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumaon">Kumaon</a> is widely believed to be a corruption of Kurmachal, and the region around Champawat became known as Kali Kumaon to distinguish it from the rest of the territory.</p><p>After the British occupation a cantonment was first set up at Champawat, but in 1815 it was moved six miles north to Lohaghat because the valley's climate was judged unhealthy. The Champawat Tiger, a man-eating tigress that killed more than four hundred people in the surrounding area, was shot by Jim Corbett in 1907, the first man-eater of his long career.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Champawat is a town and municipal board and the administrative headquarters of Champawat district, which was carved out of Pithoragarh district in 1997. It had a population of 4,801 at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> and lies on National Highway 9. Among its religious sites are the Kranteshwar temple, dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, and a temple to Ghatotkacha, whose head is believed to have fallen here after he died in the battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Champawat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to tradition the Chand dynasty was founded by Som Chand, a Rajput prince from the plains, who established himself at <b>Champawat</b> in eastern Kumaon.</blockquote><a href="/state/kumaon-kingdom">Kumaon Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
